{"kind":"report","text":"Technical phase-order decision for34ce1438 at ungated checkpoint8a0181994, clarifying9f5b0a32: YES, the proposed internal local-entry planning stage is permissible with separate admission boundaries below. There is no general requirement to duplicate native semantic selectors in SQL or create a derived index of every selected-root name merely to avoid reading already-admitted local entries. This is a narrow ordering clarification, not Source approval, a new certificate or export permission.\n\n1. Establish native caller-owned full scope/incarnation, admitted exact pinned policy/support and fixed cutoff; finish bounded whole-prefix0..through metadata classification, including the last row, before loading any local entry body for planning. Legacy/unadmitted root, foreign uses, custom domains declared by the root or encountered native owner, unsupported variant/consumer/owner/capability and incomplete/ambiguous/stopped metadata fail here. Root-pin success alone is insufficient. The trusted policy must actually cover the local entry class and its inline signed fields/evidence; a known owner name, a supplied support map, or a root digest in a set is not a new policy certification of arbitrary inline data. If safe local-entry admission cannot be established from that exact reviewed native policy and metadata, refuse/stop at that prerequisite instead of reading the disputed payload to establish its own safety.\n\n2. Only then read canonical LOCAL entries of that same selected prefix for internal semantic planning. Reserve actual returned byte sizes, decoding/canonical validation/hash and repeated selector/validation work before each operation under the original cumulative budget/deadline; bind every body to its preflight seq/hash/size/full scope and cutoff. Validate form/hash before trusted selectors consume it. Use only shared trusted native selection grammar (places/observation/evidence/owner retains), never arbitrary-string digest discovery, use